<p>Ah, it's comments like this that reaffirm that my writing did its job.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>you guys managed to take my worst fear, subvert it, turn it on its head, and make me feel sorry for not wanting to die.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My first thought when I heard this pitched to me by Othello was &quot;omg this is practically the inverse apocalypse&quot;, which I love. I adore subversion in articles, and essentially predicating the entire theme on a subversion is like my entire aesthetic. It feels too easy to try to run a grandiose battle predicated on rule of cool for this topic. Sure, that's a good way to take it, and it can feel original if done right, but I love delving into more nuanced themes.</p>
<p>Also, something appeals to me about an end of the world with no explosions, no high octane action, no violence. Just a very peculiar situation, and everyone just have to live with it.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-576267-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-576267-1')">1</a></sup></p>
